/// std/strpred — the string predicates and `slice`, written in Vyrn
/// (RFC-0078 M4b).
///
/// `contains`, `startsWith`, `endsWith`, `slice` and the `byteLength` field are
/// builtins: three implementations each (Rust in the interpreter, C in the shim
/// the textual emitter calls, hand-emitted wasm in the direct backend). RFC-0078
/// lists them as writable in Vyrn *today*, and the reason is M4a's finding
/// restated for strings — the irreducible primitive is not the operation, it is
/// the **view**. `bytes(s) -> Array<UInt8>` and `s[i] -> UInt8` already give a
/// `String` its byte reading, and `stringFromBytes(b)` gives the way back, so
/// none of the five needs to know anything a Vyrn program cannot see.
///
/// Nothing here is a *swap*: the builtins still exist and are still what user
/// code calls. These are the equivalence candidates, and
/// `examples/strpredbytes.vyrn` is the proof — it runs each one beside the
/// builtin it would replace, over the surface where two implementations can
/// differ, and asserts they agree. When the swap lands, that example becomes the
/// regression pin.
///
/// Every function is `V`-suffixed (`containsV`, `sliceV`, …) so the module can be
/// imported into a program that also calls the builtin, which is exactly what the
/// proof needs.
///
/// **Bytes, not characters.** A `String` is UTF-8 bytes and every offset and
/// length here is a byte offset, matching the builtins. That is also why the
/// predicates are safe to do byte-wise: UTF-8 is self-synchronizing — a needle's
/// first byte is either ASCII or a lead byte, never a continuation byte, and every
/// non-boundary offset in the haystack holds a continuation byte — so a valid
/// needle cannot match at a non-boundary offset. The case that looks dangerous is
/// unreachable rather than handled.
///
/// **The one place a builtin cannot be reproduced.** `slice` *traps* on a bad
/// range (`error: slice index out of range`) or a cut inside a character
/// (`error: slice splits a UTF-8 character`). Vyrn has no expression that aborts
/// with a message — there is no `panic` and no `abort` — so `sliceV` returns
/// `Option<String>` and `None` means "the builtin would trap here". A trapping
/// wrapper is one line on top of that *if* the language ever grows the primitive;
/// the honest view of a partial function is the `Option` either way, which is the
/// house idiom for the rest of the standard library.

/// The byte length of `s` — the `s.byteLength` field, as a function.
///
/// `bytes` hands over the byte view and an array knows its own length, so this
/// is the whole thing.
export fn byteLengthV(s: String) -> Int64 {
    return bytes(s).length
}

/// Does `s` begin with `needle`? An empty needle is a prefix of everything
/// (including `""`), matching the builtin.
export fn startsWithV(s: String, needle: String) -> Bool {
    let nl = byteLengthV(needle)
    if nl > byteLengthV(s) {
        return false
    }
    let mut j = 0
    while j < nl {
        if s[j] != needle[j] {
            return false
        }
        j = j + 1
    }
    return true
}

/// Does `s` end with `needle`? An empty needle is a suffix of everything.
export fn endsWithV(s: String, needle: String) -> Bool {
    let off = byteLengthV(s) - byteLengthV(needle)
    if off < 0 {
        return false
    }
    let nl = byteLengthV(needle)
    let mut j = 0
    while j < nl {
        if s[off + j] != needle[j] {
            return false
        }
        j = j + 1
    }
    return true
}

/// Does `needle` occur anywhere in `s`? An empty needle occurs at 0, so this is
/// `true` even for `""`.
///
/// The naive scan, which is what the builtin is too at these sizes.
/// `std/strings`'s `indexOf` is the same loop returning the offset — this is
/// spelled out rather than built on it so the module stays a leaf that imports
/// nothing and reaches no builtin except the byte view.
export fn containsV(s: String, needle: String) -> Bool {
    let n = byteLengthV(s)
    let nl = byteLengthV(needle)
    if nl > n {
        return false
    }
    let mut i = 0
    while i + nl <= n {
        let mut j = 0
        while j < nl && s[i + j] == needle[j] {
            j = j + 1
        }
        if j == nl {
            return true
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    return false
}

/// The bytes of `s` from `start` up to `end`, or `None` where the `slice` builtin
/// would trap: `start < 0`, `end > s.byteLength`, `start > end`, or a cut point
/// inside a multi-byte UTF-8 character.
///
/// The boundary check is not written out. A cut at a non-boundary offset either
/// starts the range on a continuation byte or ends it on a truncated character,
/// and both are invalid UTF-8 — so `stringFromBytes` refuses exactly the ranges
/// the builtin's `is_char_boundary` pair refuses, and refusing is what `None`
/// says. Both engines check the range before the boundary, and so does this.
///
/// One divergence, unreachable from ordinary source: `stringFromBytes` rejects a
/// NUL byte (RFC-0014's rule), and `slice` does not, so slicing a String that
/// contains a NUL yields `None` here and the substring there. No string literal
/// can hold one — there is no `\0` escape — and `stringFromBytes` will not build
/// one either, so the byte view is *not* a round trip only for a String no
/// program can construct through it.
export fn sliceV(s: String, start: Int64, end: Int64) -> Option<String> {
    let n = byteLengthV(s)
    if start < 0 || end > n || start > end {
        return None
    }
    let b = bytes(s)
    let mut out: Array<UInt8> = []
    let mut i = start
    while i < end {
        out.push(b[i])
        i = i + 1
    }
    return match stringFromBytes(out) {
        Ok(v) => Some(v),
        Err(e) => None,
    }
}
